open questions (could-not-judge):
  - On July 19, 2024, a faulty CrowdStrike Falcon Sensor content update caused a global IT outage by triggering an out-of-bounds memory read in the sensor's C++ code [Source: ev-1].
  - Specifically, an untrusted pointer dereference was triggered by a 21-byte input that passed through a flawed content validator due to missing input validation and a memory-safety defect [Source: ev-1].
  - The root cause of the widespread impact was monoculture concentration, where one security agent deployed at enterprise scale with fast-cadence updates became a global single point of failure [Source: ev-1].
  - The consequences were severe and far-reaching [Source: ev-1].
  - Approximately 8.5 million Windows devices were affected according to Microsoft’s estimate [Source: ev-1].
  - Disruptions extended across multiple critical sectors, including airlines (with Delta cancelling roughly 7,000 flights), banks, hospitals, 911 services, and broadcasters [Source: ev-1].
  - Financially, Delta incurred about $500M in costs from the outage [Source: ev-1].
  - In response to the incident, CrowdStrike shares fell by approximately 11% on Monday, July 22, 2024 [Source: ev-1].
  - Following the event, CEO George Kurtz testified before Congress on September 24, 2024 [ev-1] [Source: ev-1].
  - The company apologized and promised changes to its deployment processes, moving toward staged rollouts with canary testing and added sensor-level validation [Source: ev-1].
  - Additionally, Delta later sued CrowdStrike and hired David Boies as part of its legal response [Source: ev-1].
  - The consequences were severe and far-reaching. [Source: ev-1]
  - Following the event, CEO George Kurtz testified before Congress on September 24, 2024 [ev-1]. [Source: ev-1]
